Scholars describe several branches of the Marghi. The "Plain Marghi", or Marghi babal, live in the Gwoza and Damboa districts. The Marghi Putai, in the west around Damboa and Chibok, lived closest to the Kanuri and took on much of their culture; their chiefs were called Mai. Among other Marghi groups the chief is the Ptil, advised by a council of elders.

The Marghi are, above all, farmers. Being known as a great farmer has always been a man’s pride. Before colonial times families lived in round and rectangular mud houses with thatched roofs, fenced with dried corn stalks. Over the centuries the Marghi also learned to live alongside their neighbours – Kanuri, Kilba and Babur – and most Marghi people speak two or three languages.

Do you know the story of how your family or village came to Damboa? Share it with the archive below.',array[]::text[],null,false,null,'Wikipedia – Marghi people','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margi_people','[{"name":"101 Last Tribes – Margi people","url":"https://www.101lasttribes.com/tribes/margi.html"},{"name":"Neptune Prime – Brief history of Marghi, by Sunday Ijuptil Buba","url":"https://neptuneprime.com.ng/brief-history-of-marghi-by-sunday-ijuptil-buba/"}]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/marghi-plains.jpg');

The calabash trade brought money to Damboa, and money brought traders. Kanuri merchants began travelling back and forth to buy calabash. Many of them stayed, built homes and married into Marghi families. Over the generations this created the Damboa we know: many families are part Marghi and part Kanuri, while some remain fully Marghi or fully Kanuri.

Today Damboa is still sometimes called "Marghi State", and its motto, "Centre of Agriculture and Commerce", goes back to these days of farming and trade. The mixed heritage is one of the town’s strengths – a place where different peoples have long met, traded and become one community.',array[]::text[],'market',false,null,'Wikipedia – Damboa','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damboa','[{"name":"Penn Museum – Calabashes in Northern Nigeria","url":"https://www.penn.museum/sites/expedition/calabashes-in-northern-nigeria/"}]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/calabash.jpg');

These were the "Dane guns" found across West Africa. The name goes back to long-barrelled flintlock muskets that Danish and Norwegian traders brought to the coast before the mid-1800s. Over time local blacksmiths learned to make their own, and the guns replaced the bow and spear for hunting game. In a district on the edge of the Sambisa bush, hunting was part of everyday life, and hunters’ groups were respected members of the community.

The smiths’ fire, hammer and anvil also made hoes, axes and knives for farmers – the tools that fed Damboa. We would like to record this craft before it is forgotten. If your family were smiths or hunters, please tell us their story.',array[]::text[],null,false,null,'Council on Foreign Relations (citing the Wall Street Journal)','https://www.cfr.org/blog/nigerias-boko-haram-becoming-territorial','[{"name":"Wikipedia – Dane gun","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dane_gun"}]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/smiths.jpg');

From around 1970 the reserve became a safari destination. Visitors stayed in cabins and lodges to watch leopards, lions, elephants and hyenas. Bird experts recorded 62 species there, and Sambisa was once thought to be the last place in Nigeria where wild ostriches lived. In 1991 the Borno State Government made the reserve part of the Chad Basin National Park.

The forest suffered badly from poaching, tree-cutting and, from 2013, insurgency: the lodges fell down, roads became overgrown and the animals declined. But the memory of Sambisa as a place of wildlife and visitors is part of Damboa’s heritage – and a hope for the future.',array[]::text[],null,false,null,'Wikipedia – Sambisa Forest','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sambisa_Forest','[]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/sambisa.jpg');

The same year the national Local Government Reform set up local government councils as a third tier of government, and Damboa Local Government Area was created, with its headquarters in Damboa town. The reform gave elected councils responsibility for local services, while traditional rulers kept their role as custodians of culture and tradition.

The full list of Damboa’s chairmen since 1976 is being compiled from council records. If you have photos or documents from the council’s early years, please share them.',array[]::text[],null,false,null,'Daily Trust – X-raying Murtala’s 7 states at 50','https://dailytrust.com/x-raying-murtalas-7-states-at-50/','[{"name":"Wikipedia – Borno State","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borno_State"},{"name":"Wikipedia – List of governors of Borno State","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_governors_of_Borno_State"},{"name":"Wikipedia – Damboa","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damboa"}]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/lga-1976.jpg');

On 18 July 2014 the town itself was overrun. Homes were set on fire and many people were killed; eyewitnesses said half of Damboa was burnt, including the main market. Telephone lines were cut, and the National Emergency Management Agency reported that more than 15,000 people were driven from their homes, most of them to Maiduguri.

Damboa remembers everyone who lost their lives that year, and honours the soldiers, police, hunters and young volunteers who stood to defend the town. Families who wish to have a loved one remembered in this archive may contact the Information Unit.',array[]::text[],null,false,null,'Anadolu Agency','https://aa.com.tr/en/world/28-militants-killed-in-fresh-battle-with-nigeria-troops-/109101','[{"name":"Wikipedia – Damboa","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damboa"},{"name":"Wikipedia – Second Battle of Damboa","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_Battle_of_Damboa"}]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/remember-2014.jpg');

The fight was not over. In the months that followed attackers tried several times to re-enter the town and were repelled by the army’s 195 Battalion. Damboa became a garrison town, and the Maiduguri road was often dangerous.

But retaking the town was the first step on the long road home. For the thousands of Damboa families living in camps and with relatives in Maiduguri, it meant that one day they could return.',array[]::text[],null,false,null,'ICIR Nigeria – Nigerian Military Reclaims Damboa','https://www.icirnigeria.org/nigerian-military-reclaims-damboa/','[{"name":"Anadolu Agency","url":"https://aa.com.tr/en/world/28-militants-killed-in-fresh-battle-with-nigeria-troops-/109101"}]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/retaken.jpg');

People came home to burnt houses and empty streets, and began again. Families repaired their compounds, farmers went back to the land closest to town, and traders set up wherever they could. Aid agencies brought food, water and health care to returnees and to those still displaced.

Each family that returned made it easier for the next. Slowly, Damboa came back to life.',array[]::text[],null,false,null,'ICIR Nigeria – Life after Boko Haram: residents return home','https://www.icirnigeria.org/life-after-boko-haram-gradually-residents-return-home/','[]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/road-home.jpg');

He urged traders to be security-conscious and to work with the security agencies to keep the peace. The District Head of Damboa, Malam Abdullahi Damboa, said he was happy to see the market open again and pledged his support to the state government in keeping the market peaceful.

For a town built on trade, the reopening of the market was a turning point: it brought buyers from the villages, gave work to traders and porters, and showed everyone that Damboa was open for business again.',array[]::text[],'market',false,null,'Tribune Online – Damboa LG re-opens Damboa market as peace returns to Borno','https://tribuneonlineng.com/damboa-lg-re-opens-damboa-market-peace-returns-borno','[{"name":"Vanguard – Damboa market, shut after 2014 attacks, re-opens","url":"https://www.vanguardngr.com/?p=684030"},{"name":"ICIR Nigeria – Popular market near Sambisa re-opens","url":"https://www.icirnigeria.org/popular-market-close-sambisa-re-opens-borno/"}]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/market-2016.jpg');

Residents described it as a wonderful day and a clear sign that relative peace, security and stability had returned. Livestock dealers came in from the surrounding villages with cattle, sheep and goats, and townspeople expected the market to bring visitors and business back to Damboa.

The return of the Monday Market completed the story that began with the township market in 2016: Damboa was once again a centre of agriculture and commerce for southern Borno.',array[]::text[],'market',false,null,'Radio Ndarason Internationale – Excitement as popular Damboa livestock market reopens','https://ndarason.com/en/?p=52530','[]'::jsonb,'published',null,'/img/history/livestock-2024.jpg');
